      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While superchargers and two-stroke pop-pop engines can make odd noises, turbochargers are arguably unique for their ability to produce a continuous, increasing \"howl\" directly tied to engine RPM. High-performance turbos often use variable geometry or specific vane designs specifically engineered to sharpen that whistle into a musical, \"alive\" pitch rather than just a rattling sound. In an exam context, always distinguish that this specific vocalization comes from the compressor wheel and impeller vanes moving the charge at extreme speeds, whereas other whistling sounds usually stem from inert gas flow through smaller passages like petcocks.",
